(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-2-anatomy-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"Anatomy Comprehensive Master Exam — Form B","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What forms epidermal ridges, and what is their function?","choices":["Formed by the stratum basale, with dermal papillae extending into the epidermis; increases contact area between dermis and epidermis, forms skin contours (including fingerprints), and ensures a secure grip","Formed by the stratum lucidum, with epidermal pegs extending into the dermis; decreases contact area between dermis and epidermis, forms scar tissue patterns, and increases the risk of blistering","Formed by the stratum corneum, with keratin plugs extending into the dermis; has no effect on contact area between layers, forms only skin pigmentation patterns, and plays no role in grip","Formed by melanocytes, with pigment granules extending into the epidermis; increases only skin darkness in sun-exposed areas, forms freckles and moles, and has no role in grip or fingerprints"],"answer":0,"exp":"Epidermal ridges are formed by the stratum basale, with dermal papillae extending into the epidermis from the dermis; they increase the contact area between the dermis and epidermis, form the contours of the skin (including fingerprints), do not change throughout life, and ensure a secure grip. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What factors mainly determine skin color, per the material?","choices":["Only the density of hair follicles and the distribution of sweat glands across the body surface","Only the number of active sebaceous glands and the thickness of the subcutaneous fat layer","Dermal blood supply, thickness of the stratum corneum, and variable quantity of carotene and melanin","Only the pattern of dermal papillae and the density of cutaneous sensory nerve endings"],"answer":2,"exp":"Skin color is mainly due to dermal blood supply, thickness of the stratum corneum, and the variable quantity of carotene and melanin (genetically controlled). — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the difference between carotene and melanin?","choices":["Carotene is a blue-black pigment converted to vitamin D, found in melanocytes; melanin is produced/stored in keratinocytes, forms in lysosomes, and prevents bacterial skin infection","Carotene is an orange-yellow pigment converted to vitamin A, found in keratinocytes; melanin is produced/stored in melanocytes, forms in melanosomes, and prevents UV skin damage","Carotene is a colorless compound converted to vitamin C, found in fibroblasts; melanin is produced/stored in Langerhans cells, forms in mitochondria, and increases skin water loss","Carotene is a red-brown pigment converted to vitamin K, found in Merkel cells; melanin is produced/stored in keratinocytes, forms in the Golgi apparatus, and regulates body temperature"],"answer":1,"exp":"Carotene is an orange-yellow pigment that converts to vitamin A and is found in keratinocytes, while melanin is produced and stored in melanocytes (forming in melanosomes, transferred into keratinocytes, and eventually destroyed by lysosomes), preventing skin damage from UV light. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the dermis formed by, and what determines the tension (cleavage) lines and wrinkles in the skin?","choices":["A thin layer of simple squamous epithelium; the density of sweat glands determines tension lines and wrinkles","A loose layer of adipose and areolar tissue; the thickness of the subcutaneous fat determines tension lines and wrinkles","A dense layer of interlacing collagen and elastic fibers; the direction of the collagen fibers determines tension lines and wrinkles","A single layer of stratified keratinized cells; the amount of melanin pigment determines tension lines and wrinkles"],"answer":2,"exp":"The dermis is formed by a dense layer of interlacing collagen and elastic fibers, which provide skin tone and account for the skin's strength and toughness; the direction of the collagen fibers determines the tension lines (cleavage lines) and wrinkles in the skin. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What happens when the arrector pili muscles in the dermis contract?","choices":["Flattens the hair follicle and dilates sweat glands, helping them secrete watery fluid onto the skin","Relaxes the hair shaft and constricts blood vessels, helping reduce blood flow to the dermis","Loosens the hair root and stimulates melanocytes, helping them release pigment into the epidermis","Erects the hair (causing goose bumps) and compresses sebaceous glands, helping them secrete oily product onto the skin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Contraction of the arrector pili muscles erects the hair, causing goose bumps, and compresses the associated sebaceous glands, helping them secrete oily product onto the skin. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the difference between the papillary layer and reticular layer of the dermis?","choices":["Papillary layer is dense, irregular connective tissue surrounding blood vessels and nerves with interwoven collagen bundles; reticular layer is loose connective tissue containing only capillaries, lacking any nerve or gland association within the dermis","Papillary layer is composed of dense irregular connective tissue surrounding hair follicles and glands; reticular layer is loose areolar tissue containing capillaries and axons, positioned superficial to the papillary layer near the epidermis","Papillary layer is loose connective tissue containing capillaries and axons; reticular layer is dense, irregular connective tissue surrounding blood vessels/hair follicles/nerves/glands with interwoven collagen bundles extending into the subcutaneous layer","The dermis has only one undifferentiated connective tissue layer, containing scattered capillaries and axons but no distinction between superficial and deep regions, and no association with hair follicles, nerves, or glands whatsoever"],"answer":2,"exp":"The papillary layer is loose connective tissue containing capillaries and axons, while the reticular layer is dense, irregular connective tissue surrounding blood vessels, hair follicles, nerves, and glands, with interwoven collagen fiber bundles extending deep into the subcutaneous layer. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the subcutaneous tissue (superficial fascia) composed of, and what does it contain?","choices":["Keratinized epithelial cells identical to the epidermis, located superficial to the dermis; contains melanocytes, Langerhans cells, and Merkel cells, providing the body's primary barrier to water loss","Loose connective tissue and fat, located between the dermis and deep fascia; contains the deepest parts of sweat glands, blood/lymphatic vessels, and cutaneous nerves, providing most of the body's fat storage","Only epithelial cells with no connective tissue component, located between the epidermis and dermis; contains only sensory nerve endings, providing the body's sense of touch and pressure","Dense, organized connective tissue without fat, located deep to the deep fascia; contains tendons, ligaments, and periosteum, providing structural support and muscle attachment sites"],"answer":1,"exp":"The subcutaneous tissue (superficial fascia) is composed of loose connective tissue and fat, located between the dermis and underlying deep fascia; it contains the deepest parts of the sweat glands, blood and lymphatic vessels, and cutaneous nerves, and provides most of the body's fat storage (with thickness varying by nutritional state). — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What are skin ligaments, and what do they determine about skin mobility?","choices":["Numerous small fibrous bands extending through the subcutaneous tissue, attaching the dermis to the deep fascia; skin mobility over deep structures depends on their length and density","A single thick muscular band extending through the epidermis, attaching the stratum corneum to the stratum basale; skin mobility over deep structures depends on hydration and elasticity","Bundles of contractile smooth muscle fibers extending through the dermis, producing voluntary movement of the skin; skin mobility over deep structures depends on nervous stimulation frequency","A network of elastic sheets found only in the epidermis, unrelated to the subcutaneous tissue; skin mobility over deep structures depends entirely on stratum corneum thickness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Skin ligaments consist of numerous small fibrous bands that extend through the subcutaneous tissue and attach the deep surface of the dermis to the underlying deep fascia; the mobility of the skin over deep structures depends on the length and density of these ligaments. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is deep fascia, and what are its extensions called when they invest deeper structures versus divide muscles into compartments?","choices":["The outermost keratinized layer of the epidermis, enveloping only the palms and soles; dermal papillae invest deeper structures, and rete ridges divide the epidermis into segments","A dense, organized connective tissue layer without fat, enveloping most of the body deep to skin/subcutaneous tissue; investing fascia invests deeper structures, and intermuscular septa divide muscles into compartments","A loose, fat-containing layer found only in the limbs, absent from the trunk and head; superficial fascia invests deeper structures, and skin ligaments divide muscles into compartments","A thin, vascular connective tissue layer identical to subcutaneous tissue, enveloping only joints; retinacula invest deeper structures, and bursae divide muscles into compartments"],"answer":1,"exp":"Deep fascia is a dense, organized connective tissue layer without fat, enveloping most of the body deep to the skin and subcutaneous tissue; its extensions include investing fascia (investing deeper structures like individual muscles and neurovascular bundles) and intermuscular septa (dividing muscles into groups or compartments, important for compartment syndrome). — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What are retinacula and bursae, as structures formed by deep fascia?","choices":["Retinacula hold tendons in place during joint movement; bursae prevent friction and enable structures to move freely over one another","Retinacula secrete synovial fluid into joint capsules; bursae anchor ligaments to the periosteum of adjacent bones","Retinacula transmit nerve signals across joint spaces; bursae store fat deposits around moving tendons","Retinacula prevent friction between adjacent tendons during movement; bursae anchor tendons firmly in place across joints"],"answer":0,"exp":"Deep fascia also forms retinacula, which hold tendons in place during movement of joints, and bursae, which prevent friction and enable structures to move freely over one another. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What are fascial planes, and why are they important surgically?","choices":["The same fluid-filled sacs as bursae, important in preventing friction between tendons and bones during joint movement","Potential spaces between adjacent fascias or fascia-lined structures, important in surgical procedures to separate structures and create access to deeper structures","Actual, pre-existing fluid-filled spaces found only in the dermis, important in regulating interstitial fluid pressure beneath the epidermis","Dense bands of collagen fibers connecting muscle to bone, important in transmitting contractile force during movement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Fascial planes are potential spaces between adjacent fascias or fascia-lined structures; these planes are important in surgical procedures to separate structures, creating spaces that allow access to deeper structures. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"How does the skin provide containment, per the material?","choices":["Contains the tissues, organs, and vital substances of the body, preventing dehydration","By actively absorbing external fluids and electrolytes from the environment into the body","Only through the contraction of arrector pili muscles, producing goose bumps in cold weather","Only by producing sweat continuously to cool the body during physical exertion"],"answer":0,"exp":"The skin's containment function involves containing the tissues, organs, and vital substances of the body, preventing dehydration. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What does the iris consist of, and what determines eye color?","choices":["Consists of the lens and ciliary body only, with color determined by the density of the lens fibers","Consists of rods and cones, which create eye color through differing photopigment concentrations","Consists only of smooth muscle, with no pigment; eye color instead comes from the underlying choroid","Consists of blood vessels, pigment, and smooth muscles; the pigment creates eye color, and smooth muscles change pupil diameter"],"answer":3,"exp":"The iris consists of blood vessels, pigment, and smooth muscles; the pigment creates the color of the eye, and the smooth muscles contract to change the diameter of the pupil. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What does the ciliary body consist of, and what is it connected to?","choices":["The pigment layer of the retina, connected directly to the optic nerve sheath","The sclera and cornea directly, connected by a ring of collagen fibers","Only blood vessels, with no muscular component, connected to the choroid","Ciliary muscles connected to suspensory ligaments, which are connected to the lens"],"answer":3,"exp":"The ciliary body consists of ciliary muscles connected to suspensory ligaments, which are in turn connected to the lens. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is the choroid, and what does its innermost portion attach to?","choices":["A highly vascularized structure whose innermost portion attaches to the outermost portion of the retina","The pigmented outer layer of the cornea, attaching to the sclera at the limbus","An avascular structure unrelated to the retina, attaching instead to the ciliary body","A muscle controlling pupil diameter, attaching to the iris at its outer margin"],"answer":0,"exp":"The choroid is highly vascularized, and its innermost portion attaches to the outermost portion of the retina. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is the neural tunic also called, and what two layers does it consist of?","choices":["Also called the retina; consists of a pigmented layer (outer) and a neural layer (inner)","Also called the choroid; consists of only one layer, the pigmented epithelium","Also called the ciliary body; consists of muscle layers only, with no photoreceptors","Also called the sclera; consists of the cornea and lens working together"],"answer":0,"exp":"The neural tunic is also called the retina, made of two layers: the pigmented layer (outer layer) and the neural layer (inner layer). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is the difference between rods and cones in the retina?","choices":["Neither cell type is a photoreceptor","Both cell types serve identical functions","Rods are for color vision; cones are for night vision","Rods are for night vision; cones are for color vision"],"answer":3,"exp":"The retina's cells consist of rods (for night vision) and cones (for color vision). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is unique about the fovea centralis, and what happens if an object is focused on the optic disc?","choices":["Both areas have an identical rod/cone ratio, since photoreceptor distribution is uniform across the entire retina","Fovea centralis is 100% rods; optic disc is where color vision is best due to its dense ganglion cell layer","Fovea centralis is 100% cones (best color vision); optic disc has 0% rods or cones, so vision does not occur there (the \"blind spot\")","The optic disc has the highest concentration of cones in the retina, giving it the sharpest visual acuity"],"answer":2,"exp":"The fovea centralis is 100% cones, giving the best color vision when an object is focused there, while the optic disc has 0% rods or cones — if an object is focused on this area, vision does not occur, hence it is known as the \"blind spot.\" — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Why can we see color during the day but not at night, per the material's explanation?","choices":["Color vision is unrelated to rod/cone activity, depending instead entirely on wavelength sensitivity of the pigment epithelium","Rods require light to be stimulated; cones activate at night once ambient light drops below a critical threshold","Both rods and cones are equally active regardless of light level, with color perception instead governed by pupil diameter","Cones require light to be stimulated (enabling color vision); at night, cones deactivate and rods activate instead (enabling night vision without color discrimination)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cones require light to be stimulated, which is why we see color during the day; at night (with at least a small amount of light), the cones deactivate and the rods begin to be activated, allowing night vision but not color determination. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What are the anterior cavity and posterior cavity of the eye, and what fluids fill each?","choices":["Anterior cavity (anterior and posterior chambers) filled with aqueous fluid; posterior cavity (vitreous chamber) filled with vitreous fluid","Both cavities are filled with the same fluid, which circulates freely between the anterior and posterior segments","Neither cavity contains fluid; both are instead filled with a soft, collagenous connective tissue","Anterior chamber filled with vitreous gel; posterior chamber filled with watery aqueous fluid"],"answer":0,"exp":"The anterior cavity (consisting of the anterior and posterior chambers) is filled with aqueous fluid, while the posterior cavity (vitreous chamber) is filled with vitreous fluid. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the pathway of aqueous fluid (humor), from secretion to entering venous circulation?","choices":["Secreted by cells at the ciliary body → posterior chamber → through the pupil → anterior chamber → canal of Schlemm → venous circulation","Secreted by the retina → vitreous chamber → canal of Schlemm → venous circulation, bypassing the anterior chamber","Secreted by the lens → posterior chamber only, with no drainage pathway, accumulating until reabsorbed by the vitreous","Secreted by the cornea → anterior chamber only, with no further flow, remaining static until reabsorbed locally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Aqueous fluid (humor) is secreted by cells at the ciliary body, enters the posterior chamber (posterior of the iris), flows through the pupil into the anterior chamber, flows through the canal of Schlemm, and enters venous circulation. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What are the functions of vitreous fluid (humor)?","choices":["Only regulates intraocular pressure via the canal of Schlemm, with no structural role","Only lubricates the outer surface of the eye, preventing corneal drying","Produces the pigment for eye color, released from the ciliary body","Supports the shape of the eye, the position of the lens, and the position of the retina"],"answer":3,"exp":"Vitreous fluid, a gelatinous material in the posterior chamber, supports the shape of the eye, the position of the lens, and the position of the retina. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the correct order light waves travel through the eye, and how is the signal transmitted to the brain?","choices":["Retina → lens → cornea → pupil → transmitted via CN VIII, the vestibulocochlear nerve","Pupil → lens → cornea → retina → transmitted via CN I, the olfactory nerve","Cornea → lens → pupil → retina → anterior chamber → transmitted via CN IV","Cornea → anterior chamber → pupil → posterior chamber → lens → focuses on retina → depolarization → transmitted via CN II"],"answer":3,"exp":"Light waves pass through the cornea, the anterior chamber, the pupil, the posterior chamber, and the lens, which focuses the image on the retina, creating depolarization of neural cells; the signal is then transmitted to the brain via CN II (the optic nerve). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which extrinsic eye muscle elevates the upper eyelid, and which cranial nerve controls it?","choices":["Inferior rectus muscle; CN III","Superior oblique muscle; CN IV","Lateral rectus; CN VI innervates it to elevate the eyelid","Levator palpebrae superioris; CN III"],"answer":3,"exp":"The levator palpebrae superioris elevates the upper eyelid and is controlled by CN III, alongside the superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, and inferior oblique. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What structures are positioned superior to the coronary sulcus, and what is true of both atria?","choices":["The left and right ventricles; both have thick, muscular walls compared to the atria above them","The left and right atria; both have thin walls and expandable extensions called auricles (atrial appendages)","The aorta and pulmonary trunk, both lacking any expandable auricular appendages","Only the right atrium; it has no auricle, unlike the muscular left atrium beside it"],"answer":1,"exp":"The left and right atria are positioned superior to the coronary sulcus; both have thin walls and consist of expandable extensions called auricles or atrial appendages. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What three vessels does the right atrium receive deoxygenated blood from?","choices":["Pulmonary veins, pulmonary trunk, and aorta","Pulmonary trunk and pulmonary veins only","Only the inferior vena cava, entering near the diaphragm","Superior vena cava, inferior vena cava, and coronary sinus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The right atrium receives deoxygenated blood via the superior vena cava, inferior vena cava, and coronary sinus. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is the fossa ovalis, and what fetal structure is it a remnant of?","choices":["A structure found only in the left atrium","A fetal remnant of the foramen ovale","A valve structure, not a remnant of any fetal structure","A fetal remnant of the ductus arteriosus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The fossa ovalis, found in the right atrium, is a fetal remnant of the foramen ovale. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What are pectinate muscles, and what does the crista terminalis do?","choices":["Pectinate muscles are \"comb-like\" structures pushing blood unidirectionally; the crista terminalis serves as an origin of the pectinate muscles","Pectinate muscles conduct electrical impulses directly to the AV node; crista terminalis is a valve","Pectinate muscles are found in the right ventricle only; crista terminalis regulates pressure","Pectinate muscles are smooth, without any comb-like structure, and lack a defined origin point"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pectinate muscles are \"comb-like\" structures in the right atrium, structured to push blood unidirectionally; the crista terminalis serves as an origin of the pectinate muscles. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What hormone can be released if the right atrial appendage (auricle) becomes overfilled?","choices":["Aldosterone, from the adrenal cortex","Cortisol, from the adrenal cortex","Renin, from the juxtaglomerular cells","Brain (atrial) natriuretic peptide"],"answer":3,"exp":"If the right atrial appendage (auricle), which holds extra blood volume, becomes overfilled, it can stimulate the release of brain (atrial) natriuretic peptide. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Through which valve does blood pass from the right atrium into the right ventricle, and how many papillary muscles is it connected to?","choices":["Aortic valve, a semilunar valve connected to no papillary muscles or chordae tendineae whatsoever","Bicuspid (mitral) valve, connected via chordae tendineae to two papillary muscles matching its two cusps","Tricuspid valve (right AV valve), connected via chordae tendineae to three papillary muscles (matching its three cusps)","Pulmonary valve, a semilunar valve connected to four separate papillary muscles within the right ventricle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Blood enters the right ventricle by passing through the tricuspid valve (right atrioventricular valve); since there are three cusps, the chordae tendineae connect to three papillary muscles. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the moderator band, and where is it found?","choices":["A muscular band extending from the interventricular septum to the ventricular wall, containing Purkinje fibers, found only in the right ventricle","A band found only in the atria, conducting impulses directly between the SA node and AV node across the interatrial septum","A structure found equally in both ventricles, containing bundle branch fibers that conduct impulses toward the apex","A type of heart valve regulating flow between the right atrium and right ventricle, preventing any backflow of blood"],"answer":0,"exp":"The moderator band is a muscular band that extends from the interventricular septum to the ventricular wall, containing conduction tissue called Purkinje fibers; it is found only in the right ventricle, mainly serving as a conductor. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What are trabeculae carneae (\"bands of meat\"), and what is their function?","choices":["Blood vessels supplying the right ventricle's myocardium directly from the coronary circulation","A type of heart valve located between the right atrium and right ventricle","Internal surface structures of the right ventricle, structured to contract the ventricle from all directions","Structures found only in the atria, forming a comb-like ridge along the crista terminalis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Trabeculae carneae (\"bands of meat\") are internal surface structures of the right ventricle, structured to contract the ventricle from all directions. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What does the left atrium receive, and through what does blood pass to reach the left ventricle?","choices":["Receives oxygenated blood from the lungs via the pulmonary veins; blood passes through the bicuspid (mitral) valve","Receives oxygenated blood via the coronary sinus; blood passes through the pulmonary valve","Receives deoxygenated blood via the pulmonary trunk; blood passes through the tricuspid valve","Receives blood from the venae cavae directly; blood passes through the aortic valve"],"answer":0,"exp":"The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the lungs via the right and left pulmonary veins; blood passes through the bicuspid valve (left atrioventricular valve, also called the mitral valve) to reach the left ventricle. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Why does the left ventricle have the thickest myocardial tissue, described as the \"workhorse of the heart\"?","choices":["It pumps blood only a short distance through the pulmonary circuit, requiring far less force than the right ventricle actually needs","Both ventricles have identical wall thickness, since they generate equal pressure during systole regardless of circuit resistance","It needs strong contractions to pump blood throughout the entire systemic vascular circuit, unlike the right ventricle which only pumps through the pulmonary circuit","It has the thinnest walls of any chamber, since the low-resistance pulmonary circuit demands very little contractile force to move blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"The left ventricle has the thickest myocardial tissue because it needs strong contractions to pump blood throughout the entire systemic vascular circuit, while the right ventricle has a thin wall since it only pumps blood through the (shorter, lower-pressure) pulmonary circuit. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What are the two papillary muscles of the left ventricle's AV valve?","choices":["Anterolateral papillary muscle and posteromedial papillary muscle","Only one papillary muscle, matching the bicuspid valve's single cusp","Anterior and posterior papillary muscles (three total, matching three cusps)","Medial and lateral papillary muscles, four total"],"answer":0,"exp":"The left ventricle's AV valve (bicuspid/mitral) has chordae tendineae connecting to two cusps and two papillary muscles: the anterolateral papillary muscle and the posteromedial papillary muscle. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Through which valve does blood leave the left ventricle, and where does it go next?","choices":["Pulmonary valve, entering the pulmonary trunk toward the lungs","Aortic valve, entering the ascending aorta, then the aortic arch, then to all body parts (systemic)","Mitral valve, entering the left atrium in a retrograde direction","Tricuspid valve, entering the right atrium from the left side"],"answer":1,"exp":"Blood leaves the left ventricle by passing through the aortic valve, entering the ascending aorta, then traveling to the aortic arch and then to all body parts (the systemic circuit). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What are muscular arteries, and what controls their diameter?","choices":["The smallest arteries, about 30 microns in diameter, controlling blood flow directly into the capillaries","Large vessels up to 2.5 cm in diameter with a thin media; the diameter is fixed and entirely uncontrolled by any nerves","Vessels found only within the heart itself, supplying the myocardium exclusively through small branches","Medium-sized arteries up to 0.4 cm diameter with thicker media than elastic arteries; diameter is under control of the autonomic nervous system"],"answer":3,"exp":"Muscular arteries are medium-sized arteries up to 0.4 cm in diameter, with thicker media than elastic arteries; their diameter is under control of the autonomic nervous system. Examples include the radial, ulnar, external carotid, brachial, femoral, and mesenteric arteries. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What are arterioles, and what is their function?","choices":["Smallest arteries (~30 microns diameter) with very thin adventitia, controlling blood flow between arteries and capillaries","Vessels found only within the venous system, controlling the blood flow between venules and the larger veins","Vessels with no smooth muscle at all in their media, measuring up to 2.5 cm in diameter","The largest arteries, up to 2.5 cm in diameter, possessing a thick, well-developed adventitia layer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Arterioles are the smallest arteries, around 30 microns in diameter, with very thin adventitia and media that may have incomplete smooth muscle layers; they control blood flow between arteries and capillaries. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is distinctive about capillary structure, and what are the three types described?","choices":["Smallest of all vessels (~8 microns diameter), lacking adventitia and media; three types are continuous, fenestrated, and sinusoids","Only one type of capillary exists: continuous, each possessing a thick adventitia and media layer","Largest of all vessels, with a thick adventitia and media, measuring about 2.5 cm in diameter","Capillaries are identical in structure to the arterioles, each retaining a complete media and adventitia layer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Capillaries are the smallest of all vessels, about 8 microns in diameter (about the diameter of a red blood cell), and lack adventitia and media; they are the only vessels where nutrient/waste exchange occurs between blood and tissues, and there are three types: continuous (complete endothelial lining, most common), fenestrated (incomplete lining with pores), and sinusoids (discontinuous, with pores and large gaps). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What characterizes venules, and what is their function?","choices":["The largest veins, such as the superior and inferior vena cava, possessing a thick tunica media layer","Veins with the thickest tunica media of any vessel type, collecting blood from larger veins","Veins found only within the upper extremities, possessing a thick, well-developed tunica media","Smallest of the veins, collecting blood from the capillaries, lacking or having only thin tunica media"],"answer":3,"exp":"Venules are the smallest of the veins, collecting blood from the capillaries; they lack or have only a thin tunica media. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What characterizes medium-sized veins, and what are examples given?","choices":["Media is the largest layer, contains no elastic fibers, and lacks valves entirely; examples include venules only","No distinguishable layers are present at all, and no examples of this vein type are ever given in this course material","Adventitia (tunica externa) is the largest layer, contains elastic fibers, has one-way valves; examples include the radial, tibial, and popliteal veins","Adventitia is by far the thinnest layer, with no valves present anywhere at all; examples include the superior and inferior vena cava"],"answer":2,"exp":"Medium-sized veins have an adventitia (tunica externa) that is the largest of the layers, containing elastic fibers, with one-way valves present; they are found alongside muscular arteries, with examples including the radial vein, tibial vein, and popliteal vein. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Why do many medium veins have venous valves, and what is their function?","choices":["To produce hormones that regulate blood pressure throughout the entire systemic venous circulation continuously","To increase the blood flow away from the heart toward the capillary beds of the lower extremities, directly and continuously","Valves have no functional purpose in the venous system at all, existing only as vestigial evolutionary structures","To help blood in the lower extremities return to the heart against gravity; valves compartmentalize blood, acting as one-way valves to prevent backflow"],"answer":3,"exp":"Venous valves help blood returning to the heart from the lower extremities go against gravity; these valves compartmentalize the blood in the veins, acting as one-way valves that prevent backflow of blood. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What characterizes large veins, and what are examples given?","choices":["Media is by far the thickest layer with abundant smooth muscle; intima is thin; valves are present; examples include the radial and tibial veins","Large veins are identical in structure to arterioles, with a thin adventitia and a thick, muscular smooth muscle media layer throughout","Intima is by far the thickest layer, with abundant valves present; examples include the radial and popliteal veins","Adventitia is the thickest layer (with some smooth muscle); intima and media are very thin; no valves present; examples include the superior and inferior vena cava"],"answer":3,"exp":"Large veins have the thickest adventitia (containing some smooth muscle), with very thin intima and media, and no valves; examples include the superior vena cava and inferior vena cava. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What regions does the superior vena cava receive blood from, and what is the one exception to veins draining into the venae cavae?","choices":["Receives from the abdomen only; hepatic veins are the sole exception, draining via the portal vein instead","Receives from the head, neck, chest, shoulders, and upper limbs; cardiac veins are the exception (they drain via the coronary sinus instead)","Receives from the lower extremities only; the renal veins are the sole exception, draining via the aorta instead","There are no exceptions whatsoever; all veins, including cardiac veins, drain directly into the venae cavae"],"answer":1,"exp":"The superior vena cava receives blood from the head, neck, chest, shoulders, and upper limbs; all veins drain into the superior or inferior vena cava except cardiac veins, which drain via the coronary sinus into the right atrium directly. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is one described sequence for venous return from the hand to the heart via the cephalic vein?","choices":["Cephalic vein → digital veins → subclavian vein → superior vena cava directly, skipping the palmar arches","Digital veins → basilic vein → axillary vein → subclavian vein → the inferior vena cava directly, bypassing the heart's right side","Palmar venous arches → cephalic vein → the aorta → right atrium, entirely bypassing the venae cavae and subclavian vein","Digital veins → superficial palmar veins → palmar venous arches → cephalic vein → subclavian vein → brachiocephalic vein → superior vena cava"],"answer":3,"exp":"One venous return sequence is: digital veins, superficial palmar veins, palmar venous arches, cephalic vein, subclavian vein, brachiocephalic vein, and superior vena cava. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What connects the cephalic vein to the basilic vein at the elbow, in the alternate venous return pathway?","choices":["The brachial artery","The median cubital vein","The median antebrachial vein","The axillary vein directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"The median cubital vein connects the cephalic vein to the basilic vein, in the pathway where superficial palmar veins drain into the cephalic vein, then to the basilic vein, axillary vein, subclavian vein, brachiocephalic vein, and superior vena cava. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What does the azygos vein drain, and what does it drain into?","choices":["Drains into the inferior vena cava exclusively, collecting only from the left kidney","Drains the esophageal and right intercostal veins, draining into the superior vena cava","Drains only the left intercostal veins, emptying instead into the hemi-azygos vein","Drains the entire lower extremity venous system, emptying into the inferior vena cava"],"answer":1,"exp":"The azygos vein drains the esophageal and right intercostal veins, and along with the brachiocephalic veins, drains into the superior vena cava. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What do the hemi-azygos and accessory hemi-azygos veins drain?","choices":["The entire upper extremity venous system","Left superior intercostal veins","Right intercostal veins","The kidneys directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"The hemi-azygos and accessory hemi-azygos veins drain the left superior intercostal veins. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What are the three unpaired cartilages of the larynx, and what is distinctive about each?","choices":["Arytenoid, corniculate, and cuneiform cartilages, all three classified together as unpaired cartilages","Thyroid cartilage (contains laryngeal prominence), cricoid cartilage, and epiglottis (closes over the glottis during swallowing)","Only two unpaired cartilages exist in total: the thyroid and cricoid cartilages, with no epiglottis included","Vocal ligaments and vestibular ligaments, both classified here as unpaired laryngeal cartilages"],"answer":1,"exp":"The three unpaired cartilages of the larynx are the thyroid cartilage (contains the laryngeal prominence), the cricoid cartilage, and the epiglottis (closes over the glottis during swallowing of food). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What are the three paired cartilages of the larynx, and which is functionally most important?","choices":["Thyroid, cricoid, and epiglottis, all classified as paired cartilages","Vocal ligaments and vestibular ligaments (functionally most important)","Arytenoid (functionally most important), corniculate, and cuneiform cartilages","Only two paired cartilages exist: arytenoid and corniculate cartilages"],"answer":2,"exp":"The three paired cartilages of the larynx are the arytenoid cartilages (functionally most important, since skeletal muscles move them to abduct/adduct the vocal folds), corniculate cartilages, and cuneiform cartilages. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What do skeletal muscles moving the arytenoid cartilages accomplish?","choices":["Only produce mucus that lubricates the vocal folds during normal speaking","Abduct (open) the vocal folds during breathing, and adduct (close) them during speaking and swallowing","Control only the epiglottis's closure during swallowing, not the vocal folds","Only close the vocal folds during quiet breathing, with no opening function"],"answer":1,"exp":"Skeletal muscles move the arytenoid cartilages to abduct (open) the vocal folds during breathing, and to adduct (close) them during speaking and swallowing. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the difference between vocal ligaments (forming vocal folds) and vestibular ligaments (forming vestibular folds)?","choices":["Vocal ligaments are elastic, attached to the arytenoid cartilage posteriorly, forming the true vocal cords; vestibular ligaments are inelastic, forming the vestibular folds superior to the vocal folds","Vocal ligaments are inelastic, attached to the thyroid cartilage anteriorly, forming the false vocal cords; vestibular ligaments are elastic, forming folds inferior to the vocal folds","Neither ligament type is related to the vocal folds; both instead attach the epiglottis to the hyoid bone above the larynx, playing no role in phonation","Both ligament types form identical structures, each forming the same true vocal cords attached to the arytenoid cartilage posteriorly, with no distinct vestibular fold"],"answer":0,"exp":"Vocal ligaments are elastic and form the vocal folds (true vocal cords, attached to the arytenoid cartilage posteriorly), while vestibular ligaments are inelastic and form the vestibular folds, positioned superior to the vocal folds. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What does the pitch of vocal sound depend on, and why do adult males typically have deeper voices than females?","choices":["Pitch depends only on total lung volume, unrelated to vocal cord properties; at puberty, males simply develop greater lung capacity, producing a deeper voice","Males have thinner, shorter vocal cords than females; at puberty, only the cricoid cartilage thickens, which is what produces the deeper voice","Pitch depends on the diameter, length, and tension of the vocal cords; at puberty, males' vocal cords and thyroid cartilage become thicker/longer, producing a deeper voice","Pitch is unrelated to vocal cord anatomy entirely, depending instead solely on chest wall resonance, rib cage shape, and diaphragm strength"],"answer":2,"exp":"Pitch depends on the diameter, length, and tension of the vocal cords; at puberty, the vocal cords and thyroid cartilage of males become thicker and longer, producing a deeper voice than females (children have slender, short vocal folds, producing a high-pitched sound). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the difference between intrinsic and extrinsic laryngeal muscles?","choices":["Neither muscle group is related to the larynx; both instead control tongue and pharyngeal muscle movement","Both muscle groups perform an identical function, positioning and stabilizing the larynx only during swallowing","Intrinsic muscles regulate vocal cord tension and open/close the glottis; extrinsic muscles position and stabilize the larynx","Intrinsic muscles position and stabilize the larynx; extrinsic muscles regulate vocal cord tension and glottis closure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Intrinsic laryngeal muscles regulate the tension of the vocal cords and open/close the glottis, while extrinsic laryngeal muscles position and stabilize the larynx. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What are the adult trachea's dimensions, and where does it bifurcate?","choices":["11 cm long, 2.5 cm diameter; bifurcates at the carina into right and left bronchi at T5","5 cm long, 1 cm in diameter; bifurcates at the level of vertebra T12","30 cm long, 5 cm in diameter; bifurcates at the level of vertebra C6","11 cm long, 2.5 cm diameter; bifurcates at the larynx into left and right bronchi"],"answer":0,"exp":"The adult trachea is 11 cm long and 2.5 cm in diameter, bifurcating at the carina into the right and left bronchi at the level of T5. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How many tracheal cartilages does the trachea contain, and what shape are they?","choices":["Only 1 continuous spiral cartilage, not separate rings, connected by no ligaments at all","5-10 tracheal cartilages, each a complete ring, connected by loose fibrous adventitia","15-20 tracheal cartilages, each C-shaped (not complete rings), connected by annular ligaments","30-40 tracheal cartilages, each square-shaped, connected by rigid cartilaginous joints"],"answer":2,"exp":"The trachea contains 15-20 tracheal cartilages, each C-shaped (not a complete ring); annular ligaments connect one cartilage ring to another. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is the trachealis muscle, and what does it allow?","choices":["Located on the anterior side of the cartilage ring; allows only passive stretching of the trachea","Found only in the bronchioles, not the trachea; it allows bronchoconstriction and dilation there","A cartilage structure, not a muscle; it provides rigid support to the tracheal wall","Located on the posterior side of the cartilage ring; allows constriction and dilation of the trachea"],"answer":3,"exp":"The trachealis muscle is located on the posterior side of the (C-shaped) cartilage ring; it allows for constriction and dilation of the trachea. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"How does the right primary bronchus compare to the left, and what is the clinical significance?","choices":["Neither bronchus differs in any clinically relevant way, as both share an identical angle, length, and diameter","The right primary bronchus is steeper, shorter, and larger in diameter than the left, making aspiration of foreign objects into the right lung easier","Both primary bronchi are identical in angle, length, and diameter, making aspiration equally likely on either side always","The left primary bronchus is steeper, shorter, and larger in diameter than the right, making aspiration into the left lung easier"],"answer":1,"exp":"The right primary bronchus is steeper, shorter, and larger in diameter than the left primary bronchus, making it clinically easier for a person to aspirate foreign objects into the right lung than the left. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the correct branching order from primary bronchi down to bronchioles?","choices":["Primary bronchi → secondary bronchi → tertiary bronchi → bronchioles","Primary bronchi → bronchioles directly, bypassing secondary/tertiary","Bronchioles → tertiary bronchi → secondary bronchi → primary bronchi","Secondary bronchi → primary bronchi → bronchioles → tertiary bronchi"],"answer":0,"exp":"The primary bronchi branch into secondary bronchi, then tertiary bronchi, before becoming bronchioles. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the hilum, and what is the \"root of the lung\"?","choices":["Hilum is where the primary bronchus enters the lung (also the entry/exit point for pulmonary vessels); root of the lung is the combination of the bronchus, artery, and vein","Hilum and root of the lung both refer to the same single structure, the trachea, at the point where it splits into left and right bronchi","Neither term is related to lung structure; both instead describe features of the surrounding pleural membranes and mediastinal fat pad","Hilum is the apex of the lung, its most superior rounded point; root of the lung is the base, its broad inferior concave surface resting on the diaphragm"],"answer":0,"exp":"The hilum is the point where each primary bronchus enters the lung, also serving as the point of entrance and exit for the pulmonary blood vessels; the root of the lung is the combination of the bronchus, artery, and vein together. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 41"}]};
